Goal also requires dispatched shift_candidate_alpha, origin_availability_alpha, destination_availability_alpha. Dispatching requires dispatch_allocated, candidate_vehicle_linked, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked, not dispatched. So we need to allocate dispatch slot to each candidate (shift_candidate_alpha, origin_availability_alpha, destination_availability_alpha). The dispatch slot is only one: dispatch_slot_alpha. However each dispatch action consumes the dispatch_slot (makes it not available) and then after dispatch, the effect makes dispatch_slot_available again (since effect includes (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot)). Actually look at execute_*_dispatch actions: they have precondition dispatch_allocated ?candidate, candidate_vehicle_linked ?candidate ?vehicle_unit, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot, not (dispatched ?candidate). Effect: (dispatched ?candidate), (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit), (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot). So they consume the dispatch slot? Actually they have precondition candidate_dispatch_slot_linked (which was set by allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate action). The allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate action has precondition dispatch_ready ?candidate, candidate_task_profile_linked ?candidate ?route_task_profile, dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot, not dispatch_allocated ?candidate. Effect: dispatch_allocated ?candidate, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot, not dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot. So dispatch_slot becomes unavailable after allocation, and after dispatch it becomes available again. So we can reuse same dispatch slot for multiple candidates sequentially: allocate, dispatch, then slot free again.

